.TH NINJA 1 2026-10-20 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
ninja \- run the commands of a build file to bring targets up to date
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ninja
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ target ...]
.br
.B ninja
.RI [ options ]
.B \-t
.I tool
.RI [ argument ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ninja
reads a build file,
.I build.ninja
unless
.B \-f
names another, which says how to make files from other files, and runs
the commands needed to bring each
.I target
up to date.
It runs several commands at once, but starts a command only after the
commands that make its inputs have finished.
Build files are usually written by a generator such as
.BR cmake (1).
With no
.I target
operands,
.B ninja
builds the targets of the
.B default
statements, or, when there are none, every output that is not an input
of another build statement.
.PP
A
.I target
is a path as written in the build file.
It is canonicalized first: empty and
.B .\&
components are removed,
.IB dir /..\&
is collapsed, and a backslash matches a forward slash.
A
.I target
ending in
.B ^
means the first output of the first build statement that uses the named
file as an input, such as
.I main.o
for
.IR main.c^ .
.PP
A build statement runs when any of these is true:
.IP \(bu 2
An output is missing.
.IP \(bu 2
An output is older than the newest input, ignoring order-only inputs.
.IP \(bu 2
An output has no entry in the build log, or the logged command differs
from the current one, including the response file contents.
Neither applies to a
.B generator
rule.
.IP \(bu 2
The time the build log recorded for an output is older than the newest
input.
.IP \(bu 2
Discovered dependencies are missing or older than the output.
.IP \(bu 2
An input is itself out of date.
.PP
Before building, if the build file is the output of a build statement,
.B ninja
brings it up to date, and if that changed it, loads it again and starts
over.
After 100 rounds it gives up with an error.
.PP
Progress messages and the output of commands go to standard output, and
diagnostics to standard error, prefixed with
.BR "ninja: error:" ,
.BR "ninja: warning:" ,
or
.BR "ninja: fatal:" .
.SS Build files
A build file contains these statements, one per line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
\fIname\fR = \fIvalue\fR
rule \fIname\fR
build \fIoutputs\fR [| \fIimplicit\-outputs\fR]: \fIrule\fR [\fIinputs\fR] $
    [| \fIimplicit\-inputs\fR] [|| \fIorder\-only\fR] [|@ \fIvalidations\fR]
default \fItarget\fR ...
pool \fIname\fR
include \fIpath\fR
subninja \fIpath\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
Lines indented with spaces after a
.BR rule ,
.BR build ,
or
.B pool
line are
.IB name " = " value
bindings that belong to it.
Tabs are an error.
A line whose first non-space character is
.B #
is a comment; elsewhere
.B #
is an ordinary character.
Lines may end in LF or CR LF.
.PP
.B $
is the escape character:
.TP
.B $$
A literal
.BR $ .
.TP
.BR "$ " "(dollar, space)"
A literal space, which would otherwise separate paths.
.TP
.B $:
A literal colon, which would otherwise end the outputs.
.TP
.BR $ " at the end of a line"
Joins the next line, without its leading spaces.
.TP
\fB$\fIname\fR, \fB${\fIname\fB}\fR
The value of a variable.
Names consist of letters, digits,
.BR _ ,
and
.BR \- ,
and
.B .\&
is also allowed in braces.
.PP
Any other character after
.B $
is an error.
.PP
A
.B build
statement names at least one output and a rule.
Paths after the outputs'
.B |
are implicit outputs, which are not part of
.BR $out .
Inputs before any
.B |
are explicit, and form
.BR $in .
Implicit inputs, after
.BR | ,
are dependencies left out of
.BR $in .
Order-only inputs, after
.BR || ,
are built first, but a change to them does not make the statement run.
Validations, after
.BR |@ ,
are built whenever the statement is part of a build, but the statement
does not wait for them.
Two statements that make the same output are an error.
.PP
The built-in rule
.B phony
runs no command and makes its output an alias for its inputs.
A
.B phony
statement with no inputs is out of date whenever no file of that name
exists, so everything that depends on it always runs.
.PP
A
.B default
statement adds targets to the default list, and each must be a path
already mentioned earlier in the build file.
A
.B pool
statement needs a
.BI "depth = " count
binding, and limits the statements that select it with
.B pool
to
.I count
commands at once, where 0 means no limit.
The pool
.BR console ,
of depth 1, exists already.
Its command inherits
.BR ninja 's
standard input, output, and error, its output is not captured, and the
status line waits until it finishes.
.PP
.B include
reads another file into the current scope.
.B subninja
reads it into a new scope that sees the variables and rules of the
including file, but whose own definitions do not leak back.
Both paths are relative to the current directory, not to the including
file.
A rule name may be defined only once in a scope.
.SS Rules and variables
A
.B rule
statement accepts only these bindings, of which
.B command
is required.
They may also be set on a
.B build
statement.
.TP
.B command
The command line to run.
See
.B NOTES
for how it runs.
.TP
.B depfile
A Makefile-syntax dependency file, such as
.B gcc \-MD
writes, that lists more inputs.
Without
.BR deps ,
.B ninja
reads it every time it starts.
The statement is out of date when the file is missing, or when its first
target is not the statement's first output.
A target that is not an output of the statement is an error.
.TP
.B deps
How to save discovered dependencies in the deps log after the command
succeeds.
With
.BR gcc ,
.B ninja
reads
.B depfile
and deletes it, and a missing file means no dependencies;
.B depfile
must be set.
With
.BR msvc ,
it takes the lines of the command's output that start with
.B msvc_deps_prefix
and removes them from the output.
Each named file is made relative to the current directory when it is on
the same drive, and is dropped when its path contains
.B program files
or
.BR "microsoft visual studio" ,
in any case.
Before the first such line, a line ending in
.BR .c ,
.BR .cc ,
.BR .cxx ,
.BR .cpp ,
or
.BR .c++ ,
which
.B cl
prints, is also removed.
Any other value makes
.B ninja
stop with a fatal error after the command runs.
.TP
.B msvc_deps_prefix
The prefix of include lines for
.BR "deps = msvc" .
The default is
.B Note: including file:
followed by a space.
.TP
.B description
Shown in the status line instead of the command, except with
.BR \-v .
.TP
.B dyndep
A file, which must be one of the statement's inputs, that lists more
implicit inputs and outputs.
.B ninja
reads it as soon as it is up to date.
Its first statement must set
.B ninja_dyndep_version
to 1, and each build statement that names the file needs a statement
\fBbuild\fR \fIoutput\fR [\fB|\fR \fIoutputs\fR]\fB: dyndep\fR
[\fB|\fR \fIinputs\fR]
in it, where
.I output
is one of the statement's outputs, optionally followed by an indented
.B restat
binding.
.TP
.B generator
Marks a rule that generates the build file: its outputs are not rebuilt
when the command changes, and
.B "\-t clean"
keeps them unless given
.BR \-g .
.TP
.B pool
The pool the statement belongs to.
An unknown pool is an error.
.TP
.B restat
After the command succeeds, check the outputs again, and treat any
output whose time did not change as up to date, so that statements
that depend only on it do not run.
.TP
.B rspfile
A response file to write before the command runs, deleted after the
command succeeds and kept when it fails.
It must be set together with
.BR rspfile_content .
.TP
.B rspfile_content
The text of
.BR rspfile .
.PP
Any non-empty value of
.B generator
or
.BR restat ,
even
.BR 0 ,
means true.
.PP
In a rule, these variables are also defined:
.TP
.B $in
The explicit inputs, separated by spaces.
.TP
.B $in_newline
The explicit inputs, separated by newlines.
.TP
.B $out
The explicit outputs, separated by spaces.
.PP
Each path in these is canonicalized but keeps its backslashes, and is
enclosed in double quotes when it contains a space or a double quote.
Inside the quotes, a double quote and the backslashes before it are
escaped with backslashes, as the Microsoft C runtime expects.
.PP
Two variables of the top file's scope have a meaning:
.TP
.B builddir
The directory for the log files, created when needed.
.TP
.B ninja_required_version
.B ninja
stops with a fatal error when this version is newer than 1.13,
comparing the major and minor numbers, and warns when its major number
is 0.
.PP
The value of a file-level variable, or of a binding on a
.B build
statement, is expanded once when it is read.
A
.B build
statement's bindings are expanded in the file's scope, so they cannot
use
.BR $in ,
.BR $out ,
or each other.
Rule bindings are expanded when they are used, looking up each variable
in this order:
.BR $in ,
.BR $in_newline ,
and
.BR $out ;
the
.B build
statement's bindings; the rule's bindings; the file's variables; and the
variables of the files that include it with
.BR subninja .
File variables are looked up when the command runs, so a rule sees the
last value assigned in the file, even after the
.B build
statement.
For a
.B build
statement with no bindings of its own, the file's variables are looked
up before the rule's: a file-level
.B description
or
.B restat
overrides the rule's for such statements.
Rule bindings that refer to each other in a cycle are a fatal error.
.SS Status output
Each command is announced by a status line: the expansion of
.BR NINJA_STATUS ,
followed by the
.B description
or, when there is none or with
.BR \-v ,
the command.
When standard output is a console,
.B TERM
is not
.BR dumb ,
and neither
.B \-v
nor
.B \-\-quiet
is given, the status line is shown as commands start and finish,
overwritten in place, and shortened in the middle to the console width.
Otherwise one line is printed as each command finishes.
.PP
Each command's output is printed after its status line when the command
finishes, never mixed with other output.
A command that fails is reported as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
FAILED: [code=\fIstatus\fR] \fIoutput\fR ...
\fIcommand\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
followed by its output.
When nothing needs to run,
.B ninja
says there is no work to do, and when a build stops early, it says so
and why.
.SS Files
These are in the directory named by
.BR builddir ,
or in the current directory.
.TP
.I .ninja_log
The build log: for each output, a hash of its command, the command's
start and end times, and the output's time.
.TP
.I .ninja_deps
The deps log, a binary file of discovered dependencies.
.TP
.I .ninja_lock
An empty file written as commands start, to read the file system's
time, and removed afterwards.
.PP
Both logs are rewritten without stale entries when they hold many.
A log of another version is deleted with a warning, and a damaged deps
log is truncated after its last good record.
.SS Tools
.B \-t
runs a tool instead of a build.
Except for
.BR msvc ,
.BR restat ,
.BR urtle ,
and
.BR wincodepage ,
tools load the build file first, without regenerating it.
Unless noted, tools that take
.I target
operands use the default targets when there are none.
For
.BR \-h ,
the tools that have options print their usage on standard output and
exit with status 1, except
.BR msvc ,
which exits with status 0.
Other tools take
.B \-h
as an operand, and
.BR cleandead ,
.BR recompact ,
and
.B urtle
ignore their operands.
.TP
.B browse
Not available on Windows: stops with a fatal error.
.TP
\fBmsvc\fR [\fB\-e\fR \fIenvfile\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIfile\fR] \
[\fB\-p\fR \fIprefix\fR] \fB\-\-\fR \fIcommand\fR
Deprecated.
Run
.IR command ,
then copy its standard output to standard output and exit with its
status.
Its standard error is not captured.
.I command
is the text after the first
.B \-\-
with a space on each side in
.BR ninja 's
own command line.
.B \-e
reads a Windows environment block, NUL-separated
.IB name = value
strings, from
.I envfile
for the command, and also takes
.B PATH
from it.
.B \-o
removes include lines from the output, as
.B "deps = msvc"
does, with
.B \-p
as the prefix, and writes
.IB file .d\fR:
.IR file ,
a colon, and a space, then the included files one per line, which is
not a valid depfile when there is more than one.
In this build,
.B \-o
never finishes if any output line remains after the removal, even an
empty one or a compiler warning: memory use grows until it fails.
.TP
\fBclean\fR [\fB\-gr\fR] [\fItarget\fR ...]
Delete the outputs of every statement, except
.B phony
statements and
.B generator
rules, with their depfiles and response files, and print the count.
With
.I target
operands, delete those targets and every built file they depend on,
including generator outputs.
.B \-g
also deletes generator outputs.
.B \-r
takes rule names instead of targets, and deletes the outputs of their
statements.
Read-only files are deleted too.
Given
.B \-n
before
.BR \-t ,
nothing is deleted, and given
.B \-n
or
.BR \-v ,
each file is listed.
The exit status is 1 if a file could not be deleted or an operand is
unknown.
.TP
\fBcommands\fR [\fB\-s\fR] [\fItarget\fR ...]
Print the commands that build the targets, each after the commands it
depends on.
.B \-s
prints only the command of the statement that makes each target.
.TP
\fBinputs\fR [\fB\-0dE\fR] [\fItarget\fR ...]
Print every input needed to build the targets, directly or indirectly,
except outputs of
.B phony
statements, one per line, sorted, and quoted as for
.BR $in .
.BR \-0 " or " \-\-print0
ends each path with a NUL instead.
.BR \-d " or " \-\-dependency\-order
does not sort.
.BR \-E " or " \-\-no\-shell\-escape
does not quote.
.TP
\fBmulti\-inputs\fR [\fB\-0\fR] [\fB\-d\fR \fIdelim\fR] [\fItarget\fR ...]
Like
.BR inputs ,
unsorted and unquoted, but print each input as the target, a tab, and
the input.
.BR \-d " or " \-\-delimiter
replaces the tab with
.IR delim ,
and
.BR \-0 " or " \-\-print0
ends each line with a NUL.
.TP
\fBdeps\fR [\fItarget\fR ...]
Print the dependencies stored in the deps log, by default for every
output that is still built with
.BR deps .
Each entry is the output, the dependency count, the recorded time,
.B VALID
or
.BR STALE ,
and the dependencies, indented.
.TP
\fBmissingdeps\fR [\fItarget\fR ...]
Report each discovered dependency on a generated file that has no path
to its generator in the build graph, so that the target may build before
the file exists.
Exits with status 3 if there are any.
.TP
\fBgraph\fR [\fItarget\fR ...]
Print the dependency graph of the targets for Graphviz
.BR dot .
.TP
\fBquery\fR \fItarget\fR ...
Print each target's rule and inputs, with implicit inputs marked
.B |
and order-only inputs
.BR || ,
the outputs of the statements that use it, and any validations.
.TP
\fBtargets\fR [\fBdepth\fR [\fIn\fR] | \fBrule\fR [\fIname\fR] | \fBall\fR]
Print targets and their rules.
.B depth
starts from the outputs that are not inputs and shows their inputs
indented, to
.I n
levels, 1 by default, and without limit when
.I n
is 0 or less.
.BI "rule " name
prints the outputs of rule
.I name
sorted, and
.B rule
alone prints every input that no statement makes, once per use.
.B all
prints every output.
The default is
.BR "depth 1" .
.TP
\fBcompdb\fR [\fB\-x\fR] [\fIrule\fR ...]
Print a JSON compilation database of every statement with inputs, or only
of those using the named rules, giving each one's directory, command,
first input, and first output.
Without
.I rule
operands,
.B phony
statements are included, with empty commands.
.B \-x
replaces a response file named in the command after
.BR @ ,
after
.B \-f
and a space, or after
.B \-\-option\-file=
with its contents, with newlines turned into spaces.
.TP
\fBcompdb\-targets\fR [\fB\-x\fR] \fItarget\fR ...
Like
.BR compdb ,
but for the statements, except
.B phony
ones, needed to build the targets, each of which must be an output.
.TP
.B recompact
Rewrite the logs without stale entries.
.TP
\fBrestat\fR [\fIoutput\fR ...]
Record the current time of each output, or of every output, in the build
log.
It uses
.I .ninja_log
in the current directory, ignoring
.BR builddir .
.TP
\fBrules\fR [\fB\-d\fR]
Print the names of the rules of the top file's scope, which includes
files read with
.B include
but not with
.BR subninja ,
sorted.
.B \-d
adds each description, unexpanded.
.TP
.B cleandead
Delete the files that the build log lists but the build file no longer
mentions.
.TP
.B urtle
Print a turtle.
It is not listed by
.BR "\-t list" .
.TP
.B wincodepage
Print
.B "Build file encoding: UTF\-8"
if the Windows ANSI code page is UTF-8, and
.B "Build file encoding: ANSI"
otherwise.
.TP
.B list
Print the tools and exit.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may follow targets, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
Options without arguments may be grouped, as in
.BR \-nv .
An option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-j4 ,
or be the next argument, and an attached argument loses one leading
.BR = ,
so
.B \-j=4
is
.BR \-j4 .
Long options may be abbreviated to any unique prefix.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.B \-t
ends
.BR ninja 's
options, and the remaining arguments go to the tool, which parses its
own options the same way.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version, 1.13.2, and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Show full commands instead of descriptions, and print status lines
without overwriting them.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Print no status lines, and none of the
.BR "no work to do" ,
.BR "Entering directory" ,
and
.B Jobserver mode detected
messages.
Command output and failures are still printed.
.TP
.BI "\-C " dir
Change to
.I dir
before doing anything else, and print
\fBninja: Entering directory \(ga\fIdir\fB\(aq\fR
unless running a tool.
.TP
.BI "\-f " file
Read
.I file
instead of
.IR build.ninja .
.TP
.BI "\-j " n
Run up to
.I n
commands at once, where 0 means no limit.
.I n
must be a decimal integer.
The default is the number of logical processors plus 2, or 2 with one
processor and 3 with two, where a CPU rate limit on the process's job
reduces the count of processors.
.B \-j
turns off the
.B MAKEFLAGS
jobserver.
.TP
.BI "\-k " n
Keep going until
.I n
commands have failed, where 0 or less means no limit.
The default is 1.
After that, no new commands start, but running commands finish.
.TP
.BI "\-l " load
Start no more new commands at a time than
.I load
minus the current load average, rounded down, but always one when none
is running.
A
.I load
of 0 or less, the default, means no limit.
The load average is estimated: the busy fraction of all processors since
the previous check, smoothed, times the number of processors.
.TP
.B \-n
Dry run: act as if every command ran and succeeded, without running it.
Output directories are still created, and response files are still
written and deleted.
The logs are not updated.
If the build file would be regenerated,
.B ninja
exits with status 0 after that step.
.B \-n
turns off the
.B MAKEFLAGS
jobserver.
.TP
.BI "\-d " mode
Turn on a debugging mode.
.B \-d
may be repeated.
.RS
.TP
.B stats
After a build, print operation counts and times on standard output.
.TP
.B explain
Print on standard error why each command runs.
.TP
.B keepdepfile
Keep depfiles after reading them for
.BR "deps = gcc" .
.TP
.B keeprsp
Keep response files after their commands succeed.
.TP
.B nostatcache
Check the time of each file separately, instead of reading each
directory once.
.TP
.B list
Print the modes and exit.
.RE
.TP
.BI "\-t " tool
Run
.I tool
instead of building; see
.BR Tools .
.TP
.BI "\-w " flag
Adjust a warning.
.RS
.TP
.B phonycycle=err
A
.B phony
statement with one output and no implicit inputs or outputs that lists
its output as an input is a dependency cycle.
.TP
.B phonycycle=warn
Remove such an input with a warning.
This is the default.
.TP
\fBdupbuild=\fR{\fBerr\fR,\fBwarn\fR}, \fBdepfilemulti=\fR{\fBerr\fR,\fBwarn\fR}
Ignored, with a warning that they are deprecated.
.TP
.B list
Print the flags and exit.
.RE
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard error and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
When set to anything but
.BR 0 ,
turn on color even when standard output is not a console that supports
it: color escape sequences in command output are kept instead of
removed, and
.B FAILED
is shown in red.
.TP
.B MAKEFLAGS
When it contains
.BI \-\-jobserver\-auth= name\fR,
as GNU make sets it for recursive commands,
.B ninja
limits the commands it runs at once by taking a token from the Windows
semaphore
.I name
for each command after the first, instead of by the default
.B \-j
limit.
The pipe and
.B fifo:
forms, and
.BR \-\-jobserver\-fds ,
are ignored with a warning, and a semaphore that cannot be opened with
an error.
.B MAKEFLAGS
is ignored with
.B \-j
or
.BR \-n ,
or when its first word does not start with
.B \-
and contains
.BR n .
.TP
.B NINJA_STATUS
The format of the status line prefix, by default
.B [%f/%t]
followed by a space.
These placeholders are replaced:
.RS
.TP
.B %s
Commands started.
.TP
.B %t
Commands to run in total.
.TP
.B %r
Commands running.
.TP
.B %u
Commands not yet started.
.TP
.B %f
Commands finished.
.TP
.B %o
Commands finished per second, over the whole build.
.TP
.B %c
Commands finished per second, over the last
.B \-j
commands, or
.B ?\&
until known.
.TP
.B %p
Percentage of commands finished.
.TP
.B %e
Seconds elapsed, with 3 decimals.
.TP
.B %w
Time elapsed, as
.IB mm : ss
or
.IB h : mm : ss\fR.
.TP
.BR %E ", " %W
Estimated time remaining, as for
.B %e
and
.BR %w ,
based on the times in the build log, or
.B ?\&
until known.
.TP
.B %P
Estimated percentage of the total time elapsed so far.
.TP
.B %%
A literal
.BR % .
.RE
.IP
Any other placeholder, or a
.B %
at the end, is a fatal error when the first status line is printed.
.TP
.B PATH
Searched for a command's program, after
.BR ninja 's
own directory, the current directory, and the Windows system
directories.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, options end at the first argument that is not an option,
both for
.B ninja
and for its tools.
.TP
.B TERM
When
.BR dumb ,
the status line is not overwritten in place, and color is off.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including
.BR \-\-version ,
.BR "\-t list" ,
and most tools.
.TP
.B 1
An error: an invalid option or build file, an unknown target, a fatal
error,
.BR \-h ,
.BR "\-d list" ,
or
.BR "\-w list" .
.TP
.B 3
.B "\-t missingdeps"
found missing dependencies.
.TP
.B 130
The build was interrupted with Ctrl-C or Ctrl-Break, or a command exited
with
.BR STATUS_CONTROL_C_EXIT .
.PP
When commands fail,
.B ninja
exits with the status of the last one that failed, which may be any
value, and is 1 when the program was not found.
.SH EXAMPLES
A build file that compiles and links a C program with
.BR gcc ,
recording each object's headers in the deps log:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
cflags = \-O2 \-Wall
rule cc
  command = gcc \-MMD \-MF $out.d $cflags \-c \-o $out $in
  deps = gcc
  depfile = $out.d
  description = CC $out
rule link
  command = gcc \-o $out $in
  description = LINK $out
build main.o: cc main.c
build util.o: cc util.c
build app.exe: link main.o util.o
.EE
.RE
.PP
Build it with four commands at once:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ninja \-j4
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show why commands would run, without running them:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ninja \-n \-d explain
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the command that builds one file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ninja \-t commands \-s util.o
gcc \-MMD \-MF util.o.d \-O2 \-Wall \-c \-o util.o util.c
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show where a file fits in the graph:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ninja \-t query main.o
main.o:
  input: cc
    main.c
  outputs:
    app.exe
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write a compilation database for the C sources:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ninja \-t compdb cc >compile_commands.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the files that cleaning would delete, then delete them:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ninja \-n \-t clean
$ ninja \-t clean
.EE
.RE
.PP
Build in the directory
.I build
with the percentage done and the elapsed time in the status line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ NINJA_STATUS=\(aq[%p %w] \(aq ninja \-C build
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Commands are started with
.BR CreateProcess ,
not through a shell, so redirection, pipes, and other shell syntax need
.B "cmd /c"
or
.BR "sh \-c" .
.B CreateProcess
looks for the program in
.BR ninja 's
own directory, which is the kit's
.IR bin ,
the current directory, and the Windows system directories before
.BR PATH .
A command whose program is not found fails with the output
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
CreateProcess failed: The system cannot find the file specified.
.EE
.RE
.PP
but when other commands run at the same time or later,
.B ninja
may instead stop with a fatal error from
.BR ReadFile .
Any other failure to start a command, such as a command line longer than
32767 characters, is a fatal error, so use a response file for long
lists.
.PP
Commands outside the
.B console
pool get the null device as standard input, and one pipe for both
standard output and standard error, so they do not see a console and
often turn off color unless told otherwise.
They run in their own process group.
On Ctrl-C or Ctrl-Break,
.B ninja
sends Ctrl-Break to each of them, deletes the outputs that the
interrupted commands changed, or all their outputs and the depfile when
the rule has a depfile, and exits with status 130.
.PP
This build of
.B ninja
has no application manifest, so it uses the ANSI code page: paths in the
build file, in depfiles, and in
.I target
operands are in that code page, which is UTF-8 only when Windows is set
to use UTF-8 for it.
A path longer than 260 characters that does not start with a backslash
fails with
.BR "Filename longer than 260 characters" .
.PP
Paths are compared byte for byte, so
.I foo.c
and
.I Foo.c
are different targets even when they name the same file.
File times have a resolution of 100 nanoseconds.
Response files are written in text mode, so each line ends in CR LF.
.PP
The kit patches
.B ninja
so that recovery from a damaged deps log removes the damaged record.
Upstream Ninja 1.13.2 can keep it, and then rebuilds on every run.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cmake (1),
.BR cmcldeps (1),
.BR gcc (1),
.BR make (1)
